      {
        "id": "def-rpm-collision-objects",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Finite event collisions and observer quantities",
        "statement": "Use the flat oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski model, signature $(-,+,+,+)$, and SI convention $x^0=ct$ of [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]]. A **relativistic-particle-mechanics collision configuration** is a mass-specified causal collision datum of [[def-sr-collision-datum-and-conservation]]: an event $p\\in A$, a finite nonempty incoming list $P_1,\\ldots,P_N\\in V$, and a finite nonempty outgoing list $P'_1,\\ldots,P'_K\\in V$, where every leg is a nonzero future-causal vector in the same displacement space $V$. A massive leg has a recorded mass $m>0$ and the massive-sector momentum of [[def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy]]; a null leg has recorded mass $0$ and is a nonzero future-null vector with its scale specified independently. The definition does not identify a null momentum with $mU$ at $m=0$.\n\nThe side totals are the finite vector sums $Q_{\\rm in}:=\\sum_{i=1}^N P_i$ and $Q_{\\rm out}:=\\sum_{j=1}^K P'_j$. All legs are attached to the same event, so the sums compare vectors in one affine displacement space; no transport between different events is part of the datum. The adjective **isolated** is reserved for a model condition used by the postulate below: no external four-momentum transfer during the idealized event and no omitted incoming or outgoing leg.\n\nAt an event, an **observer** is a future unit timelike vector $n$ with $\\eta(n,n)=-1$, and its rest space is $n^\\perp=\\{v\\in V:\\eta(v,n)=0\\}$, as in [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]]. Observer energy $E_n(P):=-c\\eta(P,n)$ and spatial momentum $q_n(P):=P+\\eta(P,n)n\\in n^\\perp$ are those of [[def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy]]. Their units are joules and kilogram metres per second. An inertial laboratory means one named inertial chart and its time-axis observer; it is not a general observer congruence or an arbitrary coordinate chart.",
        "deps": [
          "def-sr-collision-datum-and-conservation",
          "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline",
          "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-sr-collision-datum-and-conservation": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Dynamics and Relativity, Chapter 7 Special Relativity lecture notes",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel7.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Taylor and Wheeler, Spacetime Physics, 2nd ed., Chapter 7, Momenergy",
              "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Relativity/Spacetime_Physics_%28Taylor_and_Wheeler%29/07%3A_Momenergy/7.06%3A_Conservation_of_Momenergy_and_Its_Consequences"
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": "Finite event-local collision data for classical point particles and ideal null legs in the adopted flat relativistic-particle model; no interaction law, collision occurrence, probability, or curved-spacetime total-energy claim.",
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "post-rpm-isolated-collision-balance",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Isolated event four-momentum balance",
        "statement": "For the relativistic-particle-mechanics collision configurations of [[def-rpm-collision-objects]], adopt the isolated-collision balance already stated in clause (iii) of [[post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics]]: when the event is isolated and the incoming and outgoing lists contain every leg, $Q_{\\rm in}=Q_{\\rm out}$. This local postulate records the exact collision interface used on this page; it is a scoped reuse of the existing adopted model assumption, not a second independent conservation assumption.\n\nThe balance is an adopted physical premise. It does not show that any specified reaction occurs and supplies no interaction mechanism, selection rule, probability, cross section, or amplitude. The cited treatments motivate and explain the model formulation; they do not turn the postulate into a deductive theorem or a report of an observed collision.",
        "deps": [
          "def-rpm-collision-objects",
          "post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-rpm-collision-objects": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "dependency_level": 7,
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Dynamics and Relativity, Chapter 7 Special Relativity lecture notes",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel7.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Taylor and Wheeler, Spacetime Physics, 2nd ed., Chapter 7, Momenergy",
              "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Relativity/Spacetime_Physics_%28Taylor_and_Wheeler%29/07%3A_Momenergy/7.06%3A_Conservation_of_Momenergy_and_Its_Consequences"
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": "Scoped reuse in relativistic-particle mechanics of the existing special-relativity isolated-collision postulate, for finite complete event data in flat Minkowski spacetime; no collision occurrence, mechanism, probability, or rate is asserted.",
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-rpm-system-mass-threshold",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "System invariant mass and final-state thresholds",
        "statement": "Let a mass-specified isolated collision configuration of [[def-rpm-collision-objects]] satisfy the balance postulate [[post-rpm-isolated-collision-balance]]. Write its common total as $Q:=Q_{\\rm in}=Q_{\\rm out}$. It is nonzero future causal, and it is timelike if either side contains a massive leg. If $Q$ is future timelike, let $M:=\\sqrt{-\\eta(Q,Q)}/c$, and let $\\mu_{\\rm in}$ and $\\mu_{\\rm out}$ be the sums of the recorded rest masses on the two sides. Then\n\n$$M\\ge\\mu_{\\rm in},\\qquad M\\ge\\mu_{\\rm out}.$$\n\nIn particular, a proposed final collection with total rest mass $\\mu$ requires the invariant squared energy $\\sigma:=-c^2\\eta(Q,Q)=M^2c^4$ to satisfy $\\sigma\\ge\\mu^2c^4$. If an actual final collection contains a nonzero null leg, then the inequality is strict. Equality is kinematically attained exactly when every leg on the relevant side is massive and all its four-momenta are proportional to the same future unit timelike vector. This is a necessary kinematic condition; it does not establish that the interaction realizes the channel.\n\nIf instead $Q$ is future null, each side consists only of null legs on one common future null ray; no center-of-momentum frame exists and no positive-mass final leg is compatible with that total. In SI units $m_i,M,\\mu$ are kilograms, $Mc^2$ is joules, and $\\sigma$ is joules squared.",
        "deps": [
          "def-rpm-collision-objects",
          "post-rpm-isolated-collision-balance",
          "thm-rpm-finite-future-momentum-sum",
          "pthm-rpm-inverse-momentum-and-work",
          "def-sr-total-four-momentum-and-invariant-mass"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-rpm-collision-objects": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-rpm-isolated-collision-balance": "physical-assumption",
