      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-rpm-fixed-target-threshold",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Fixed-target invariant energy threshold",
        "statement": "In a named inertial laboratory frame, let a massive projectile of rest mass $m_1>0$ and energy $E_1$ strike a massive target of rest mass $m_2>0$ initially at rest. Let the incoming momenta be $P_1=(E_1/c,\\mathbf p_1)$ and $P_2=(m_2c,\\mathbf 0)$, and let $\\mu\\ge0$ be the sum of the rest masses in a proposed final state of a finite isolated collision. Then\n\n$$\\sigma:=-c^2\\eta(P_1+P_2,P_1+P_2)=m_1^2c^4+m_2^2c^4+2m_2c^2E_1.$$\n\nThe necessary final-state threshold from [[pthm-rpm-system-mass-threshold]] is $\\sigma\\ge\\mu^2c^4$, hence\n\n$$E_1\\ge\\frac{(\\mu^2-m_1^2-m_2^2)c^2}{2m_2},\\qquad E_1\\ge m_1c^2.$$\n\nIf a nonzero null final leg is required, the invariant-mass inequality is strict, $\\sigma>\\mu^2c^4$; the displayed invariant threshold can then be an unattained endpoint. These are kinematic necessities, not guarantees of a physical channel.\n\nIn SI units $m_1,m_2,\\mu$ are kilograms, $E_1$ is joules, and $\\sigma$ is joules squared.",
        "deps": [
          "pthm-rpm-system-mass-threshold",
          "pthm-rpm-inverse-momentum-and-work",
          "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
