      {
        "id": "pthm-rpm-two-null-invariant-energy",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Two null momenta and collision angle",
        "statement": "Let $P_1,P_2$ be two nonzero future-null incoming momenta in a finite collision configuration, and let $n$ be the future unit observer of a named inertial laboratory frame. Define $E_i:=E_n(P_i)>0$, $\\mathbf p_i:=q_n(P_i)$, and equip $n^\\perp$ with the induced Euclidean inner product $\\langle u,v\\rangle_n:=\\eta(u,v)$ and norm $|u|_n:=\\sqrt{\\langle u,u\\rangle_n}$. Let $\\theta\\in[0,\\pi]$ be their spatial angle, $\\cos\\theta=\\langle\\mathbf p_1,\\mathbf p_2\\rangle_n/(|\\mathbf p_1|_n|\\mathbf p_2|_n)$. For $Q:=P_1+P_2$ and $\\sigma:=-c^2\\eta(Q,Q)$,\n\n$$\\sigma=2E_1E_2(1-\\cos\\theta),\\qquad M(Q)=\\frac{\\sqrt{\\sigma}}{c^2}.$$\n\nThe total $Q$ is null exactly when $\\theta=0$ (the two incoming vectors lie on one future null ray); it is timelike for $\\theta>0$. In particular, for head-on momenta $\\theta=\\pi$, $M(Q)=2\\sqrt{E_1E_2}/c^2$; if $E_1=E_2=E$, this is $2E/c^2$. The angle and the two energies depend on the named observer, while $\\sigma$ and $M(Q)$ are invariant.\n\nIn SI units $E_1,E_2$ are joules, $\\sigma$ is joules squared, and $M(Q)$ is kilograms; $\\theta$ is dimensionless.",
        "deps": [
          "def-rpm-collision-objects",
          "def-sr-total-four-momentum-and-invariant-mass",
          "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy",
          "lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities",
          "lem-sr-future-causal-sums-and-inertial-coordinate-criterion"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-rpm-collision-objects": "formulation-prerequisite",
